window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-binomial","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Binomial Theorem · Practice","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The expansion of $\\left(\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{x}{2}\\right)^5$ has no term independent of $x$.\nReason (R): The power of $x$ in the general term of this expansion is $2r - 5$, which is odd for every integer $r$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^{5-r}\\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^r$.\nThe power of x is $r - (5 - r) = 2r - 5$, so R is true.\nAn odd number is never 0, so no term is free of x. Hence A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The fourth term in the expansion of $(1-2x)^5$ is","image":"","options":["$-80x^3$","$80x^3$","$-40x^3$","$40x^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The fourth term is $T_4 = T_{3+1}$, so $r = 3$.\n$T_4 = {}^5C_3 (1)^{2} (-2x)^3$.\n$= 10 \\times (-8x^3) = -80x^3$.\nThe sign is negative because $(-2x)$ is raised to an odd power.\n$40x^2$ is the third term ($r=2$), not the fourth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In some row of Pascal's triangle (index $n$), three consecutive entries $^nC_{r-1}$, $^nC_r$, $^nC_{r+1}$ are in the ratio $1:3:5$. Find $n$.","image":"","options":["6","7","8","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $\\frac{^nC_r}{^nC_{r-1}} = \\frac{n-r+1}{r} = 3$, which gives $n = 4r - 1$.\nUse $\\frac{^nC_{r+1}}{^nC_r} = \\frac{n-r}{r+1} = \\frac{5}{3}$, which gives $3n = 8r + 5$.\nSubstituting the first into the second: $12r - 3 = 8r + 5$, so $r = 2$ and $n = 7$.\nCheck: $^7C_1, {}^7C_2, {}^7C_3 = 7, 21, 35$, which are in the ratio $1:3:5$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $(a+b)^4$, written in decreasing powers of $a$, which of the following is the fourth term?","image":"","options":["$4ab^3$","$6a^2b^2$","$4a^3b$","$b^4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Pattern: the power of $a$ falls by 1 and the power of $b$ rises by 1 in each successive term. The indices always add up to 4.\nThe terms run $a^4, a^3b, a^2b^2, ab^3, b^4$, so the fourth term contains $ab^3$.\nThe row of Pascal's triangle for index 4 is 1, 4, 6, 4, 1, so its fourth coefficient is 4. The fourth term is $4ab^3$.\n$6a^2b^2$ is the third (middle) term, and $4a^3b$ is the second term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match the terms of List-I with their coefficients in List-II for the expansion of $\\left(1 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^4$.\nList-I:\n(P) $x^2$\n(Q) $x$\n(R) $x^{-1}$\n(S) $x^{-2}$\nList-II:\n(1) $16$\n(2) $\\frac{1}{2}$\n(3) $8$\n(4) $-4$","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The coefficient of $x^{j-k}$ is $\\sum \\frac{4!}{i!j!k!}(-1)^k 2^{k-j}$.\n(P) $x^2$: $(j,k)=(2,0)$ gives $\\frac{3}{2}$; $(3,1)$ gives $-1$; total $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n(Q) $x$: $(1,0)$ gives $2$; $(2,1)$ gives $-6$; total $-4$.\n(R) $x^{-1}$: $(0,1)$ gives $-8$; $(1,2)$ gives $24$; total $16$.\n(S) $x^{-2}$: $(0,2)$ gives $24$; $(1,3)$ gives $-16$; total $8$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(1 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^4$, $x \\neq 0$, is:","image":"","options":["$-5$","$7$","$-11$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $\\frac{4!}{i!j!k!}1^i\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^j\\left(-\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^k$ with $i+j+k=4$. The power of $x$ is $j-k$.\nA constant term needs $j = k$. Using $\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{2}{x}\\right) = -1$:\n$(j,k)=(0,0)$ gives 1.\n$(1,1)$ gives $\\frac{4!}{2!1!1!}(-1) = -12$.\n$(2,2)$ gives $\\frac{4!}{2!2!}(+1) = 6$.\nSum: $1 - 12 + 6 = -5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For every positive integer n, $6^n - 5n - 1$ is divisible by 25.\nStatement II: For every positive integer n, $6^n - 5n - 1$ is divisible by 125.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Expanding: $6^n = (1+5)^n = 1 + 5n + 25\\,{}^nC_2 + 125\\,{}^nC_3 + \\dots$\nSo $6^n - 5n - 1 = 25({}^nC_2 + 5\\,{}^nC_3 + \\dots)$.\nStatement I: Correct, because the right side is a multiple of 25.\nStatement II: False. For n = 2, $36 - 10 - 1 = 25$, which is not divisible by 125.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the simplified form of $(x+y)^5 - (x-y)^5$, the coefficient of $x^2y^3$ is","image":"","options":["20","10","0","40"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a difference, only the odd powers of y survive, and each surviving term doubles.\nSo the result is $2(^5C_1x^4y + {^5C_3}x^2y^3 + {^5C_5}y^5)$.\nThe coefficient of $x^2y^3$ is $2\\times{^5C_3} = 2\\times10 = 20$. Option (2) forgets the doubling.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, where n is a positive integer, is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The expansion has $(n+1)$ terms.","In every term, the sum of the indices of a and b equals n.","The index of b in the third term is 3.","The coefficients ${}^nC_r$ are called binomial coefficients."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the number of terms is one more than the index.\n(2) Correct: in a general term $a^{n-r}b^r$, the indices add up to $(n-r)+r = n$.\n(3) Incorrect: the index of b starts at 0 in the first term. So it is 2 in the third term, whose general form is ${}^nC_2a^{n-2}b^2$.\n(4) Correct: this is the standard name for these coefficients.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the expansion of $(2x-3)^6$. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The expansion has 7 terms","The 4th term is the middle term","The last term is $-729$","The first term is $64x^6$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the number of terms is $n+1 = 7$.\n(2) Correct: with 7 terms, the middle one is the 4th.\n(3) Incorrect: the last term is $(-3)^6 = +729$, since an even power is positive.\n(4) Correct: the first term is $(2x)^6 = 64x^6$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the expansion of $(x-y)^n$, the terms are alternately positive and negative (taking $x, y > 0$).\nReason (R): $(-y)^r$ is positive when $r$ is even and negative when $r$ is odd.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $(x-y)^n = \\sum {}^nC_r\\, x^{n-r}(-y)^r$.\nR is true: an even power of $-y$ is positive and an odd power is negative.\nThe term number $r$ goes 0, 1, 2, ..., so the signs go +, −, +, −, ... This makes A true.\nThe alternation comes directly from the factor $(-y)^r$, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$^5C_0+{}^5C_1+{}^5C_2+{}^5C_3+{}^5C_4+{}^5C_5=32$","$^5C_0-{}^5C_1+{}^5C_2-{}^5C_3+{}^5C_4-{}^5C_5=0$","$\\sum_{r=0}^{5}2^r\\,{}^5C_r=243$","$\\sum_{r=0}^{5}3^r\\,{}^5C_r=729$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Use $(1+x)^5=\\sum {}^5C_r x^r$.\n(1) Correct: at $x=1$ the sum is $2^5=32$.\n(2) Correct: at $x=-1$ the sum is $0^5=0$.\n(3) Correct: at $x=2$ the sum is $3^5=243$.\n(4) Incorrect: at $x=3$ the sum is $4^5=1024$, not $729$. The value $729 = 3^6$ comes from mixing up the base and the index.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(x + \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^6$ is","image":"","options":["15","20","6","30"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r\\, x^{6-r}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^r = {}^6C_r\\, x^{6-2r}$.\nFor the term independent of x: $6 - 2r = 0$, so $r = 3$.\n$T_4 = {}^6C_3 = 20$.\nThe value 15 is ${}^6C_2$, which is the coefficient of $x^2$, not the constant term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the rule that each row of Pascal's triangle begins and ends with 1 and every inner entry is the sum of the two entries just above it, which of the following is the row for index 6?","image":"","options":["1 6 15 20 15 6 1","1 6 12 18 12 6 1","1 5 10 10 5 1","1 6 15 15 6 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Row for index 5: 1 5 10 10 5 1.\nAdd adjacent pairs: 1+5 = 6, 5+10 = 15, 10+10 = 20, 10+5 = 15, 5+1 = 6.\nPlace 1 at each end to get 1 6 15 20 15 6 1, which has 7 terms, one more than the index.\nOption (4) has only 6 terms, and option (3) is the row for index 5.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Using $(50+1)^4$, the value of $(51)^4$ is 6765201.\nStatement II: Using $(50-1)^4$, the value of $(49)^4$ is 5765201.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The terms needed are $50^4 = 6250000$, $4\\cdot50^3 = 500000$, $6\\cdot50^2 = 15000$, $4\\cdot50 = 200$ and $1$.\nStatement I: $6250000 + 500000 + 15000 + 200 + 1 = 6765201$. Correct.\nStatement II: $6250000 - 500000 + 15000 - 200 + 1 = 5764801$, not 5765201. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"To expand $(3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2)^3$ with the binomial theorem, it is written as $[3x^2 + (3a^2 - 2ax)]^3$. After simplification, the term ${}^3C_1(3x^2)^2(3a^2 - 2ax)$ of this binomial expansion equals:","image":"","options":["$81a^2x^4 - 54ax^5$","$27a^2x^4 - 18ax^5$","$81a^2x^4 + 54ax^5$","$54a^2x^4 - 36ax^5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given term: ${}^3C_1(3x^2)^2(3a^2-2ax)$.\n${}^3C_1 = 3$ and $(3x^2)^2 = 9x^4$, so the term is $27x^4(3a^2 - 2ax)$.\nMultiplying out: $81a^2x^4 - 54ax^5$.\nOption (2) leaves out the factor 3 from ${}^3C_1$. Option (3) has the wrong sign on the $x^5$ term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the relation ${}^{k}C_r + {}^{k}C_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$ used in the inductive proof of the binomial theorem, find the value of ${}^{7}C_3 + {}^{7}C_2$.","image":"","options":["35","56","84","28"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $k = 7$ and $r = 3$.\nFormula: ${}^{7}C_3 + {}^{7}C_2 = {}^{8}C_3$.\nSubstituting: ${}^{8}C_3 = \\frac{8\\cdot7\\cdot6}{3\\cdot2\\cdot1} = 56$.\nCheck directly: $35 + 21 = 56$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The approximation of $(0.98)^4$ obtained from the first three terms of its binomial expansion, namely 0.9224, is slightly greater than the true value of $(0.98)^4$.\nReason (R): In $(1-x)^4$ with $x = 0.02$, the remaining terms $-4x^3 + x^4$ add up to a negative quantity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expansion: $(1-x)^4 = 1 - 4x + 6x^2 - 4x^3 + x^4$.\nFirst three terms: $1 - 0.08 + 6(0.0004) = 0.9224$.\nR: the remaining terms are $-4(0.000008) + 0.00000016 = -0.00003184$, which is negative. So R is true.\nA: the true value is $0.9224 - 0.00003184 = 0.92236816$, which is less than 0.9224. So A is true.\nA holds precisely because the neglected part is negative, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the expansion of $(2x+3y)^5$, the coefficient of $x^2y^3$ is 1080.\nReason (R): The term containing $x^2y^3$ in $(2x+3y)^5$ uses the Pascal's triangle entry 10, multiplied by $2^2$ and $3^3$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The term with $x^2y^3$ is the fourth term, and its Pascal entry from row 1 5 10 10 5 1 is 10.\nThe term is $10(2x)^2(3y)^3$, so R is true.\nIts coefficient is $10 \\times 4 \\times 27 = 1080$, so A is true.\nR shows exactly how that coefficient arises, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In Pascal's triangle, the row for index 5 is 1 5 10 10 5 1. Each new row is built by adding adjacent entries of the previous row and placing 1 at both ends. Which of the following is the row for index 6?","image":"","options":["1 6 15 20 15 6 1","1 5 15 20 15 5 1","1 6 15 15 6 1","1 6 16 20 16 6 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Place 1 at the start of the row.\nAdd adjacent pairs of the index-5 row: 1+5 = 6, 5+10 = 15, 10+10 = 20, 10+5 = 15, 5+1 = 6.\nPlace 1 at the end, giving 1 6 15 20 15 6 1.\nThis row has 7 entries, one more than the index, as it should. Option (3) has only 6 entries, so it cannot be the row for index 6.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The expansion of $(2x+3y)^9$ has 10 terms.\nStatement II: In every term of the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, the sum of the indices of $a$ and $b$ equals $n$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $(a+b)^n$ has $n+1$ terms. For $n=9$ there are 10 terms, so Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: The general term is ${}^nC_k a^{n-k}b^k$. The indices add to $(n-k)+k=n$ in every term, so Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $^9C_1+{}^9C_3+{}^9C_5+{}^9C_7+{}^9C_9$ is","image":"","options":["128","256","512","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let $E$ be the sum of the even-index coefficients and $O$ the sum of the odd-index coefficients.\nPutting $x=1$ in $(1+x)^9$: $E+O=2^9=512$.\nPutting $x=1$ in $(1-x)^9$: $E-O=0$.\nSubtracting the second equation from the first: $2O=512$, so $O=256$.\nOption (3) is the total of all the coefficients, not just the odd-index ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The middle term in the expansion of $\\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{10}$ is","image":"","options":["252","$-252$","210","$-210$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Here $n = 10$, so there are 11 terms. The middle term is the 6th term ($r = 5$).\n$T_6 = {}^{10}C_5\\, x^{5}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^5 = 252 \\times (-1)^5 = -252$.\nThe option 252 ignores the sign: $(-1)^5$ is negative.\nThe value 210 is ${}^{10}C_4$, which belongs to the 5th term, not the middle one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $(1 + x)\\left(\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{x}{2}\\right)^5$ is","image":"","options":["0","20","$-20$","40"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term of the second factor: ${}^5C_r\\, 2^{5-2r}(-1)^r x^{2r-5}$. Its powers of x are always odd, so it has no constant term.\nThe constant term of the product therefore comes only from x times the $x^{-1}$ term.\nFor $x^{-1}$: $2r - 5 = -1$, so $r = 2$, giving $10 \\cdot 2^{1} \\cdot 1 = 20$.\nSo the required term is $1 \\times 0 + 20 = 20$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $(1+x)^3(1-x)^4$ is","image":"","options":["3","$-3$","1","$-1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Group the factors: $(1+x)^3(1-x)^4 = [(1+x)(1-x)]^3(1-x) = (1-x^2)^3(1-x)$.\nExpand: $(1-x^2)^3 = 1 - 3x^2 + 3x^4 - x^6$.\nMultiplying by $(1-x)$, an $x^3$ term can only come from $(-3x^2)(-x) = 3x^3$.\nSo the coefficient of $x^3$ is 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the proof of $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} {}^nC_r\\, a^{n-r} b^r$ by mathematical induction. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The base case checks $(a+b)^1 = {}^1C_0\\, a + {}^1C_1\\, b$.\n(ii) When $(a+b)$ multiplies the assumed expansion of $(a+b)^k$, the coefficient of $a^{k-1}b^2$ in the product is ${}^kC_2 + {}^kC_1$.\n(iii) The last term ${}^kC_k\\, b^{k+1}$ is rewritten as ${}^{k+1}C_{k+1}\\, b^{k+1}$ using ${}^kC_k = 1 = {}^{k+1}C_{k+1}$.\n(iv) The identity used to combine the middle coefficients is ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r+1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $P(1)$ gives $a + b$, so the base case holds.\n(ii) Correct: $a \\cdot {}^kC_2\\, a^{k-2}b^2$ and $b \\cdot {}^kC_1\\, a^{k-1}b$ both give $a^{k-1}b^2$, so the coefficient is ${}^kC_2 + {}^kC_1$.\n(iii) Correct: both coefficients equal 1.\n(iv) Incorrect: ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r+1} = {}^{k+1}C_{r+1}$, not ${}^{k+1}C_r$. The rule used in the proof is ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The middle entry of the row for index 8 of Pascal's triangle is 70.\nStatement II: The row for index 7 of Pascal's triangle has exactly one middle entry.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: the row for index 8 has 9 entries, so its single middle entry is the 5th one.\nThat entry is $^8C_4 = \\frac{8!}{4!\\,4!} = 70$, so Statement I is true.\nStatement II: the row for index 7 has 8 entries, an even number.\nIt therefore has two middle entries, $^7C_3 = {}^7C_4 = 35$, so Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The expansion of $(a+b)^4$ has 5 terms.\n(ii) In the expansion of $(a+b)^3$, the third term is $3a^2b$.\n(iii) In every term of the expansion of $(a+b)^4$, the index of $a$ plus the index of $b$ equals 4.\n(iv) In every term of the expansion of $(x^2+y)^3$, the index of $x$ plus the index of $y$ equals 3.\n(v) Adding adjacent entries of the row 1, 1 (index 1) and putting 1 at both ends gives the row 1, 2, 1 for index 2.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the number of terms is one more than the index, so $4+1=5$.\n(ii) Incorrect: the terms of $(a+b)^3$ are $a^3, 3a^2b, 3ab^2, b^3$. So the third term is $3ab^2$, not $3a^2b$.\n(iii) Correct: the indices of $a$ and $b$ in each term add up to $n = 4$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the rule applies to the indices of the quantities $x^2$ and $y$, not of $x$ itself. The terms are $x^6, 3x^4y, 3x^2y^2, y^3$, so the index sums of $x$ and $y$ are 6, 5, 4, 3.\n(v) Correct: $1+1=2$, with a 1 at each end, gives 1, 2, 1.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(\\sqrt{5}+1)^4 + (\\sqrt{5}-1)^4$ is","image":"","options":["56","112","$48\\sqrt{5}$","96"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $a=\\sqrt5$, $b=1$.\nFormula: $(a+b)^4+(a-b)^4 = 2[a^4+6a^2b^2+b^4]$, because the odd-power terms cancel.\nSubstituting: $a^4=25$ and $6a^2b^2=6\\times5=30$, so the value is $2[25+30+1]=2\\times56$.\nSo the value is 112. Option 56 forgets the factor 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Taking $a = 1$ in the expansion of $(3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2)^3$, the sum of the coefficients of all the terms containing even powers of $x$ (including the constant term) is:","image":"","options":["$288$","$-224$","$64$","$512$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $f(x) = (3x^2 - 2x + 3)^3 = \\sum c_r x^r$.\n$f(1) = (3 - 2 + 3)^3 = 64$.\n$f(-1) = (3 + 2 + 3)^3 = 512$.\nThe even-power sum is $\\frac{f(1) + f(-1)}{2} = \\frac{576}{2} = 288$.\nCheck: $27 + 117 + 117 + 27 = 288$.\nThe value $-224$ is the odd-power sum, $\\frac{64 - 512}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms are there in the expansion of $(a+b)^4$?","image":"","options":["4","5","6","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the expansion of $(a+b)^n$ has one more term than the index n.\nHere n = 4, so the number of terms is 4 + 1 = 5.\nCheck: $a^4 + 4a^3b + 6a^2b^2 + 4ab^3 + b^4$ has 5 terms.\nOption 4 is tempting, but it equals the index itself, not the number of terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"To evaluate $(997)^4$, a student writes it as $(1000-3)^4$ and expands it using the binomial theorem. How many terms of this expansion are negative?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The expansion has $4+1 = 5$ terms. The general term is ${}^4C_k (1000)^{4-k}(-3)^k$.\nThe sign is negative only when $k$ is odd, so the signs run $+, -, +, -, +$.\nThe negative terms are $k = 1$ and $k = 3$, which gives 2 terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The remainder when $6^{12} - 60$ is divided by 125 is","image":"","options":["1","26","51","76"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Expanding: $6^{12} = (1+5)^{12} = 1 + 12(5) + {}^{12}C_2(25) + 125[{}^{12}C_3 + \\dots]$.\nSo $6^{12} - 60 = 1 + 66 \\times 25 + 125p = 1 + 1650 + 125p$.\nReducing: $1650 = 125 \\times 13 + 25$.\nHence $6^{12} - 60 = 125(p + 13) + 26$, and the remainder is 26.\nOption (1), 1, comes from stopping at mod 25 and ignoring the $25\\,{}^nC_2$ term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (expression, divisor) with List-II (remainder).\nList-I:\n(A) $2^{2027}$ divided by 7\n(B) $9^{10} - 80$ divided by 64\n(C) $6^{21}$ divided by 25\n(D) $5^{99}$ divided by 13\nList-II:\n(I) 1\n(II) 4\n(III) 6\n(IV) 8","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $2^{2027} = 4 \\cdot 8^{675} = 4(7+1)^{675} = 4(7k+1)$, so the remainder is 4, giving II.\n(B) $9^{10} - 80 = (9^{10} - 8 \\cdot 9 - 9) + 1 = 64k + 1$, giving I.\n(C) $6^{21} - 5(21) = 25k + 1$, so $6^{21} = 25k + 106 = 25(k+4) + 6$, giving III.\n(D) $5^{99} = 5(26-1)^{49} = 5(13m - 1) = 13 \\cdot 5m - 5$, and $-5 \\equiv 8$, giving IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following evaluations are correct?\n(i) $(11)^5 = 161051$\n(ii) $(999)^3 = 997002999$\n(iii) $(105)^3 = 1157625$\n(iv) $(52)^3 = 140508$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $(10+1)^5 = 100000 + 50000 + 10000 + 1000 + 50 + 1 = 161051$.\n(ii) Correct: $(1000-1)^3 = 10^9 - 3\\times10^6 + 3000 - 1 = 997002999$.\n(iii) Correct: $(100+5)^3 = 1000000 + 150000 + 7500 + 125 = 1157625$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $(50+2)^3 = 125000 + 15000 + 600 + 8 = 140608$.\nSo 3 evaluations are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For n = 2, the expression $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9$ equals $64k$. What is the value of k?","image":"","options":["9","10","11","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2, so the expression is $9^3 - 16 - 9$.\nSubstituting: $729 - 25 = 704$.\nDividing: $704 \\div 64 = 11$.\nThis agrees with the binomial result that $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9$ is always a multiple of 64.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (term of $(x+2)^6$) with List-II (its coefficient).\nList-I:\n(A) $x^5$\n(B) $x^4$\n(C) $x^2$\n(D) $x$\nList-II:\n(P) 192\n(Q) 60\n(R) 12\n(S) 240","image":"","options":["A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The coefficient of $x^{6-r}$ is ${}^6C_r 2^r$.\n(A) $x^5$, $r=1$: $6 \\times 2 = 12$ → R.\n(B) $x^4$, $r=2$: $15 \\times 4 = 60$ → Q.\n(C) $x^2$, $r=4$: $15 \\times 16 = 240$ → S.\n(D) $x$, $r=5$: $6 \\times 32 = 192$ → P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) $(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2)^4-(\\sqrt3-\\sqrt2)^4$\n(Q) $(\\sqrt2+1)^6+(\\sqrt2-1)^6$\n(R) Number of terms in the expansion of $(2x+3y)^{12}$\n(S) Middle binomial coefficient in the expansion of $(a+b)^6$\nList-II\n(1) 198\n(2) 20\n(3) $40\\sqrt6$\n(4) 13","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1","P-1, Q-3, R-2, S-4","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) $(a+b)^4-(a-b)^4 = 8ab(a^2+b^2)$. With $a=\\sqrt3$, $b=\\sqrt2$: $8\\sqrt6 \\times 5 = 40\\sqrt6$, so P-3.\n(Q) $(x+1)^6+(x-1)^6 = 2(x^6+15x^4+15x^2+1)$. At $x=\\sqrt2$: $2(8+60+30+1) = 198$, so Q-1.\n(R) The number of terms is $12+1 = 13$, so R-4.\n(S) The middle coefficient is ${}^6C_3 = 20$, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In Pascal's triangle, the row for index 5 is 1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1. Using the rule that forms each new row from the previous one, what is the third entry (from the left) in the row for index 6?","image":"","options":["10","15","20","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: each inner entry of a row is the sum of the two entries just above it.\nThe third entry of row 6 lies below the 2nd and 3rd entries of row 5, which are 5 and 10.\nSo the entry is 5 + 10 = 15. This agrees with ${}^6C_2 = 15$.\n20 is the middle entry ${}^6C_3$, not the third one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the binomial theorem, the remainder when $7^{103}$ is divided by 25 is","image":"","options":["7","18","24","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $7^2 = 49 = 50 - 1$.\nRewriting: $7^{103} = 7 \\cdot (49)^{51} = 7(50 - 1)^{51}$.\nExpanding: every term of $(50-1)^{51}$ except $(-1)^{51}$ contains 50, a multiple of 25. So $(50-1)^{51} = 25m - 1$.\nSubstituting: $7^{103} = 7(25m - 1) = 25(7m) - 7 = 25(7m - 1) + 18$.\nSo the remainder is 18.\nOption (1), 7, wrongly ignores the sign of $(-1)^{51}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The coefficient of $x^4$ in the expansion of $(2x-3)^6$ is 2160.\nReason (R): In the expansion of $(ax+b)^n$, the coefficient of $x^{n-r}$ is ${}^nC_r\\, a^{n-r} b^r$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $T_{r+1} = {}^nC_r (ax)^{n-r} b^r$, so the coefficient of $x^{n-r}$ is ${}^nC_r a^{n-r} b^r$.\nApply R with $n=6$, $a=2$, $b=-3$. For $x^4$ we need $r=2$.\nCoefficient $= 15 \\times 2^4 \\times (-3)^2 = 15 \\times 16 \\times 9 = 2160$, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^4$ in the expansion of $(1+x)(1-2x)^5$ is","image":"","options":["0","160","-80","80"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $(1+x)(1-2x)^5 = (1-2x)^5 + x(1-2x)^5$.\nSo the coefficient of $x^4$ = [coefficient of $x^4$ in $(1-2x)^5$] + [coefficient of $x^3$ in $(1-2x)^5$].\nCoefficient of $x^4$: ${}^5C_4(-2)^4 = 5 \\times 16 = 80$.\nCoefficient of $x^3$: ${}^5C_3(-2)^3 = 10 \\times (-8) = -80$.\nTotal $= 80 - 80 = 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the expansion of $(3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2)^3$, the coefficient of $a^r x^{6-r}$ equals the coefficient of $a^{6-r} x^r$ for every $r = 0, 1, \\ldots, 6$.\nReason (R): The expression $3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2$ remains unchanged when $x$ and $a$ are interchanged.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: swapping $x$ and $a$ turns $3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2$ into $3a^2 - 2xa + 3x^2$, which is the same expression.\nSo the cube is also symmetric in $x$ and $a$, and its coefficients must be palindromic.\nCheck A with the actual expansion: $27x^6 - 54ax^5 + 117a^2x^4 - 116a^3x^3 + 117a^4x^2 - 54a^5x + 27a^6$. The coefficients are symmetric, so A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the binomial theorem, compare $101^{50}$ with $100^{50} + 99^{50}$. Which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$101^{50} > 100^{50} + 99^{50}$","$101^{50} < 100^{50} + 99^{50}$","$101^{50} = 100^{50} + 99^{50}$","The comparison cannot be made without computing both sides fully"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $101 = 100 + 1$ and $99 = 100 - 1$.\nIn $(100+1)^{50} - (100-1)^{50}$, the even-position terms cancel and the odd-position terms double:\n$= 2[{}^{50}C_1 100^{49} + {}^{50}C_3 100^{47} + \\dots]$.\nAll terms in the bracket are positive, so the difference $> 2 \\times 50 \\times 100^{49} = 100^{50}$.\nHence $101^{50} - 99^{50} > 100^{50}$, which gives $101^{50} > 100^{50} + 99^{50}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(1.1)^{10000} > 1000$.\nStatement II: The third term in the binomial expansion of $(1 + 0.1)^{10000}$ is 499950.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $(1+0.1)^{10000} > 1 + 10000(0.1) = 1001 > 1000$. True.\nStatement II: third term $= {}^{10000}C_2 (0.1)^2$.\n${}^{10000}C_2 = \\frac{10000 \\times 9999}{2} = 49995000$.\nSo the third term $= 49995000 \\times 0.01 = 499950$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the binomial theorem, the remainder when $9^{10}$ is divided by 64 is","image":"","options":["17","1","9","33"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write $9^{10} = (1+8)^{10} = 1 + {}^{10}C_1(8) + {}^{10}C_2(8^2) + \\ldots$\nEvery term from ${}^{10}C_2\\,8^2$ onwards contains $64$, so each is divisible by 64.\nThat leaves $9^{10} = 1 + 80 + 64k = 81 + 64k$.\nSince $81 = 64 + 17$, the remainder is 17.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the binomial theorem for a positive integer $n$ is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The last term in the expansion of $(x-y)^n$ is $(-1)^n y^n$","The inductive step of the proof uses the identity ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$","In the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, the index of $a$ in the second term is $n-1$","In the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, the index of $b$ in the $r$th term is $r$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the last term is ${}^nC_n(-y)^n = (-1)^n y^n$.\n(2) Correct: like terms are grouped using Pascal's rule, ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$.\n(3) Correct: the index of $a$ starts at $n$ and decreases by 1, so it is $n-1$ in the second term.\n(4) Incorrect: the index of $b$ starts at 0 in the first term, so in the $r$th term it is $r-1$, not $r$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"While proving $(1.01)^{1000000} > 10000$, NCERT writes $(1+0.01)^{1000000} = 1 + 1000000 \\times 0.01 + \\text{other positive terms}$. Why may the 'other terms' be dropped when concluding that the expression exceeds 10000?","image":"","options":["Every remaining term is ${}^nC_r (0.01)^r$ with a positive coefficient and a positive base, so dropping them can only decrease the sum","Every remaining term is smaller than 0.01, so together they are negligible","The remaining terms alternate in sign and cancel each other","The expansion of $(1+0.01)^{1000000}$ contains only two terms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each term is ${}^nC_r (1)^{n-r}(0.01)^r$. Here ${}^nC_r > 0$ and $(0.01)^r > 0$, so every term is positive.\nDropping positive terms gives a smaller quantity. Hence the full sum is greater than $1 + 10000 = 10001$, which is greater than 10000.\nOption 2 fails because these terms are not small. For example, the third term is about $5\\times10^7$.\nAlternating signs occur only for $(1-x)^n$. The expansion has $10^6 + 1$ terms.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(2x - \\frac{1}{3x^2}\\right)^9$, $x \\neq 0$, is","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1792}{9}$","$\\frac{1792}{9}$","$-\\frac{448}{3}$","$\\frac{448}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^9C_r (2x)^{9-r}\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^r x^{-2r}$.\nThe power of $x$ is $9-r-2r = 9-3r$. Setting $9-3r = 0$ gives $r = 3$.\n$T_4 = {}^9C_3 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{27}\\right) = 84 \\times 64 \\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{27}\\right)$.\n$= -\\frac{5376}{27} = -\\frac{1792}{9}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"If the sum of all the binomial coefficients in the expansion of $(1+x)^n$ is 1024, then $n$ equals","image":"","options":["9","10","11","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The binomial coefficients are $^nC_0, {}^nC_1, \\dots, {}^nC_n$.\nPutting $x=1$ in $(1+x)^n$ gives $\\sum {}^nC_r = 2^n$.\nSo $2^n = 1024 = 2^{10}$, which gives $n=10$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For any positive integer $n$, ${}^nC_0 - {}^nC_1 + {}^nC_2 - \\dots + (-1)^n\\,{}^nC_n = 0$.\nReason (R): The expansion of $(1-x)^n$ is ${}^nC_0 - {}^nC_1x + {}^nC_2x^2 - \\dots + (-1)^n\\,{}^nC_nx^n$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Put $a=1$ and $b=-x$ in the binomial theorem to get this expansion.\nA is true. Put $x=1$ in R: the left side becomes $(1-1)^n = 0$, and the right side becomes the alternating sum of the binomial coefficients.\nSo A follows directly from R, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the binomial theorem, find the remainder when $6^{13}$ is divided by 25.","image":"","options":["1","16","6","11"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $6^{13}=(1+5)^{13}={}^{13}C_0+{}^{13}C_1\\cdot5+5^2(\\dots)$.\nEvery term from the third onward contains $5^2=25$, so all of them are multiples of 25.\nThis gives $6^{13}=1+65+25k=66+25k$.\nSince $66=2\\times25+16$, the remainder is 16.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"To show that $a - b$ is a factor of $a^n - b^n$, we write $a^n = [(a-b) + b]^n$ and expand it. Which term of this expansion does NOT contain the factor $(a-b)$?","image":"","options":["$^nC_0 (a-b)^n$","$^nC_1 (a-b)^{n-1} b$","$^nC_{n-1} (a-b) b^{n-1}$","$^nC_n b^n$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Expanding: $[(a-b)+b]^n = {}^nC_0(a-b)^n + {}^nC_1(a-b)^{n-1}b + \\dots + {}^nC_{n-1}(a-b)b^{n-1} + {}^nC_n b^n$.\nThe power of $(a-b)$ falls from n down to 0. Only in the last term is it 0.\nThat last term is ${}^nC_n b^n = b^n$.\nSo $a^n - b^n = (a-b)[(a-b)^{n-1} + \\dots + {}^nC_{n-1} b^{n-1}]$.\nOption (3) still contains $(a-b)$ to the first power, so it does not qualify.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the proof of the binomial theorem by mathematical induction:\n(i) P(1) is verified as $(a+b)^1 = {}^{1}C_0 a + {}^{1}C_1 b$.\n(ii) In the inductive step, $(a+b)^{k+1}$ is written as $(a+b)(a+b)^k$.\n(iii) After grouping like terms, the coefficient of $a^{k+1-r}b^r$ (for $1 \\le r \\le k$) is ${}^{k}C_r + {}^{k}C_{r-1}$.\n(iv) The identity used to combine coefficients is ${}^{k}C_r + {}^{k}C_{r-1} = {}^{k}C_{r+1}$.\n(v) The last term uses ${}^{k}C_k = 1 = {}^{k+1}C_{k+1}$.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the base case, and it gives $a + b$.\n(ii) Correct: the induction hypothesis is applied to $(a+b)^k$.\n(iii) Correct: the term $a^{k+1-r}b^r$ arises from $a \\cdot {}^{k}C_r a^{k-r}b^r$ and from $b \\cdot {}^{k}C_{r-1}a^{k-r+1}b^{r-1}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: Pascal's rule gives ${}^{k}C_r + {}^{k}C_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$, not ${}^{k}C_{r+1}$.\n(v) Correct.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"If $(1+\\sqrt{2})^5 = A + B\\sqrt{2}$, where A and B are integers, then $A + B$ equals","image":"","options":["70","140","58","99"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The conjugate gives $(1-\\sqrt2)^5 = A - B\\sqrt2$.\nAdding: $2A = 2[1 + {^5C_2}\\cdot2 + {^5C_4}\\cdot4] = 2(1 + 20 + 20)$, so $A = 41$.\nSubtracting: $2B\\sqrt2 = 2\\sqrt2[{^5C_1} + {^5C_3}\\cdot2 + {^5C_5}\\cdot4] = 2\\sqrt2(5 + 20 + 4)$, so $B = 29$.\nCheck: $A^2 - 2B^2 = 1681 - 1682 = -1 = (1-2)^5$.\nSo $A + B = 70$. Option (2) uses the doubled values $2A$ and $2B$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(1.01)^{1000000} > 10000$.\nReason (R): In the expansion of $(1+0.01)^{1000000}$, every term is positive, and the first two terms add up to $1 + 1000000\\times0.01$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: every term ${}^nC_k(0.01)^k$ is positive, and the first two terms are $1+10^6\\times0.01=1+10000$.\nA follows directly from R: the sum is $10001 + \\text{(positive terms)} > 10000$.\nSo A is true and R correctly explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms are there in the expansion of $(2x - 3y)^{9}$?","image":"","options":["9","10","11","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $(2x-3y)^9$, which has the form $(a+b)^n$ with $a = 2x$, $b = -3y$ and $n = 9$.\nFact: $(a+b)^n$ has $(n+1)$ terms, one more than the index.\nThe signs and constants inside the brackets do not change the number of terms.\nSo the number of terms = 9 + 1 = 10.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms in the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^5$, $x \\neq 0$, contain a negative power of $x$?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r (x^2)^{5-r}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^r = {}^5C_r\\, 2^r x^{10-3r}$, with $r = 0, 1, \\dots, 5$.\nThe powers of $x$ for $r = 0$ to $5$ are $10, 7, 4, 1, -2, -5$.\nOnly $r = 4$ and $r = 5$ give negative powers, so there are 2 such terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct for $n = 6$?\n(i) ${}^6C_0+{}^6C_1+{}^6C_2+\\dots+{}^6C_6 = 64$\n(ii) ${}^6C_0-{}^6C_1+{}^6C_2-\\dots+{}^6C_6 = 0$\n(iii) ${}^6C_2 = {}^6C_4$\n(iv) The expansion of $(a+b)^6$ has exactly 6 terms","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: putting $x=1$ in $(1+x)^6$ gives $2^6=64$.\n(ii) Correct: putting $x=1$ in $(1-x)^6$ gives $0$.\n(iii) Correct: ${}^6C_2=15={}^6C_4$, since ${}^nC_r={}^nC_{n-r}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: there are $6+1=7$ terms.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})^6 + (\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})^6$ is an integer.\nReason (R): In this sum, terms with odd powers of $\\sqrt{2}$ cancel, and each remaining term has even powers of both $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. The odd-k terms cancel. For even k, the power of $\\sqrt3$ is $6-k$, which is also even.\nSo every surviving term is rational, which explains A.\nThe sum is $2[(\\sqrt3)^6 + 15(\\sqrt3)^4(\\sqrt2)^2 + 15(\\sqrt3)^2(\\sqrt2)^4 + (\\sqrt2)^6]$.\nThis equals $2[27 + 270 + 180 + 8] = 970$, an integer, so A is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(\\sqrt{2}+1)^4 + (\\sqrt{2}-1)^4$ is","image":"","options":["17","34","24","68"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: $(a+b)^4 + (a-b)^4 = 2(a^4 + 6a^2b^2 + b^4)$.\nPut $a = \\sqrt{2}$ and $b = 1$: $a^4 = 4$, $a^2b^2 = 2$, $b^4 = 1$.\nSum $= 2(4 + 12 + 1) = 2\\times17 = 34$.\nCheck: $(\\sqrt2\\pm1)^2 = 3\\pm2\\sqrt2$, and squaring again gives $17\\pm12\\sqrt2$, which add to 34.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the expansion of $\\left(x^2-\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^6$, $x\\neq0$.\nStatement I: The term independent of $x$ is 240.\nStatement II: The coefficient of $x^6$ is $-60$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The general term is ${}^6C_r(x^2)^{6-r}\\left(-\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^r={}^6C_r(-2)^rx^{12-3r}$.\nStatement I: Setting $12-3r=0$ gives $r=4$. The term is ${}^6C_4\\cdot16=15\\times16=240$, so Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: Setting $12-3r=6$ gives $r=2$. The coefficient is ${}^6C_2\\cdot(-2)^2=15\\times4=+60$, not $-60$, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the expansion of $(x+a)^n + (x-a)^n$, every term containing an odd power of $a$ cancels out.\nStatement II: The simplified form of $(x+a)^6 - (x-a)^6$ contains a term in $x^6$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The term $^nC_k x^{n-k} a^k$ appears as $+$ in the first expansion and as $(-1)^k$ times it in the second. For odd k the two cancel when added.\nStatement II is incorrect. In a difference, the even-k terms cancel instead. The $x^6$ term has k = 0, so it disappears.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Coefficient of $x^3$ in $(x+2)^6$\n(B) Coefficient of $x^4y$ in $(2x+3y)^5$\n(C) Coefficient of $x^2y^3$ in $(x-2y)^5$\n(D) Coefficient of $x^5$ in $\\left(x^2+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^4$\nList-II\n(P) 12\n(Q) $-80$\n(R) 160\n(S) 240","image":"","options":["A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-P, B-S, C-Q, D-R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) The $x^3$ term is ${}^6C_3x^3\\cdot2^3$, so the coefficient is $20\\times8=160$ (R).\n(B) The term is ${}^5C_1(2x)^4(3y)$, so the coefficient is $5\\times16\\times3=240$ (S).\n(C) The term is ${}^5C_3x^2(-2y)^3$, so the coefficient is $10\\times(-8)=-80$ (Q).\n(D) The general term is ${}^4C_r3^rx^{8-3r}$. Setting $8-3r=5$ gives $r=1$, so the coefficient is $4\\times3=12$ (P).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $(3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2)^3$, the coefficient of $x^3$ is:","image":"","options":["$-116a^3$","$-108a^3$","$-124a^3$","$116a^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A term $(3x^2)^i(-2ax)^j(3a^2)^k$ with $i+j+k=3$ carries $x^{2i+j}$.\nWe need $2i + j = 3$:\n$(i,j,k) = (1,1,1)$: $3!\\cdot 3\\cdot(-2a)\\cdot 3a^2 = -108a^3$.\n$(0,3,0)$: $(-2a)^3 = -8a^3$.\nTotal: $-116a^3$.\nOption (2) forgets the $(-2ax)^3$ term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(1.02)^{500} > 10$.\nReason (R): For $x > 0$ and any positive integer $n$, $(1+x)^n \\ge 1 + nx$, because all terms after the second in the binomial expansion are non-negative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + {}^nC_2x^2 + \\dots$. For $x > 0$ every extra term is $\\ge 0$, so R is true.\nA: Using R, $(1.02)^{500} \\ge 1 + 500 \\times 0.02 = 11 > 10$. So A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Find the value of $\\sum_{k=0}^{6} {}^{6}C_k\\, 3^{6-k} (-1)^{k}$.","image":"","options":["64","4096","729","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compare with $\\sum_{k=0}^{n} {}^{n}C_k a^{n-k} b^k = (a+b)^n$.\nHere $a = 3$, $b = -1$ and $n = 6$.\nSo the sum = $(3 - 1)^6 = 2^6 = 64$.\nThe option 4096 equals $(3+1)^6$, which ignores the factor $(-1)^k$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following expressions are divisible by $64$ for every positive integer $n$?\n(i) $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9$\n(ii) $3^{2n+2} - 8n - 9$\n(iii) $9^n - 8n - 1$\n(iv) $9^n - 8n - 9$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Start with $9^n = (1+8)^n = 1 + 8n + 64\\,({}^nC_2 + 8\\,{}^nC_3 + \\dots)$.\n(iii) Divisible: $9^n - 8n - 1 = 64m$.\n(i) Divisible: $9^{n+1} = 1 + 8(n+1) + 64m'$, so $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9 = 64m'$.\n(ii) Divisible: $3^{2n+2} = 9^{n+1}$, so this is the same expression as (i).\n(iv) Not divisible: $9^n - 8n - 9 = 64m - 8$. For $n = 1$ it equals $-8$.\nSo three of the expressions qualify.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following numbers appear in the row for index 9 of Pascal's triangle?\n36, 84, 120, 126, 72","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The row for index 9 is $^9C_0, \\dots, {}^9C_9$.\nIts values are 1, 9, 36, 84, 126, 126, 84, 36, 9, 1.\nFrom the list, 36, 84 and 126 appear in this row.\n120 is $^{10}C_3$, which belongs to the row for index 10, and 72 does not appear at all.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the Pascal's triangle row 1 5 10 10 5 1, find the coefficient of $x^3y^2$ in the expansion of $(2x+3y)^5$.","image":"","options":["90","360","720","1080"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The term containing $x^3y^2$ is the third term, and its Pascal entry is 10.\nTerm: $10(2x)^3(3y)^2$.\nSubstituting: $10 \\times 8 \\times 9 = 720$.\nSo the coefficient is 720. The value 1080 belongs to $x^2y^3$, and 90 forgets the powers of 2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $17^n - 4^n$ is divisible by $13$ for every positive integer $n$.\nReason (R): If $a$ and $b$ are distinct integers, then $a - b$ is a factor of $a^n - b^n$ for every positive integer $n$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Write $a^n = (a-b+b)^n = b^n + {}^nC_1 (a-b) b^{n-1} + \\dots + (a-b)^n$.\nThen $a^n - b^n = (a-b)\\,[\\,{}^nC_1 b^{n-1} + \\dots + (a-b)^{n-1}\\,]$, which is a multiple of $a-b$.\nA is true. Put $a = 17$ and $b = 4$ in R. Then $17 - 4 = 13$ divides $17^n - 4^n$.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are equal to $2^{10}$?\n(i) ${}^{10}C_0 + {}^{10}C_1 + \\dots + {}^{10}C_{10}$\n(ii) $\\sum_{r=0}^{5} 3^r\\,{}^{5}C_r$\n(iii) ${}^{11}C_0 + {}^{11}C_2 + {}^{11}C_4 + \\dots + {}^{11}C_{10}$\n(iv) $\\sum_{r=0}^{5} 2^r\\,{}^{5}C_r$\n(v) The sum of the coefficients in the expansion of $(3x-1)^{10}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Equal: the sum of all coefficients of index 10 is $2^{10}$.\n(ii) Equal: the sum is $(1+3)^5 = 4^5 = 2^{10}$.\n(iii) Equal: adding $(1+1)^{11}$ and $(1-1)^{11}$ shows that twice the even-indexed sum is $2^{11}$, so the sum is $2^{10}$.\n(iv) Not equal: the sum is $(1+2)^5 = 3^5 = 243$.\n(v) Equal: put $x = 1$ to get $(3-1)^{10} = 2^{10}$.\nSo four of them are equal to $2^{10}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(x^2 - \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^6$, $x \\neq 0$, is","image":"","options":["240","-240","160","60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r (x^2)^{6-r}\\left(-\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^r = {}^6C_r(-2)^r x^{12-3r}$.\nFor the term independent of $x$, set $12-3r=0$, which gives $r=4$.\nTerm $= {}^6C_4(-2)^4 = 15 \\times 16 = 240$.\nIt is positive because $r=4$ is even.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms are there in the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{3}{x}\\right)^7$?","image":"","options":["7","8","9","14"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The expansion of $(a+b)^n$ has $(n+1)$ terms, one more than the index.\nHere $a = x^2$, $b = \\frac{3}{x}$ and $n = 7$.\nSo the number of terms is $7 + 1 = 8$.\nThe option 14 is a trap. It comes from confusing the power of x in the first term ($x^{14}$) with the number of terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (terms in the expansion of $(x+2)^4$) with List-II (coefficients).\nList-I:\n(A) Coefficient of $x^3$\n(B) Coefficient of $x^2$\n(C) Coefficient of $x$\n(D) Constant term\nList-II:\n(i) 16\n(ii) 32\n(iii) 8\n(iv) 24","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use the row 1 4 6 4 1. The power of x falls from 4 to 0 while the power of 2 rises from 0 to 4.\n$x^3$: $4\\cdot 2 = 8$, so A-(iii).\n$x^2$: $6\\cdot 2^2 = 24$, so B-(iv).\n$x$: $4\\cdot 2^3 = 32$, so C-(ii).\nConstant: $2^4 = 16$, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^5$ in the expansion of $(3x^2 - 2ax + 3a^2)^3$ is:","image":"","options":["$-54a$","$54a$","$-18a$","$-27a$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write the expression as $[3x^2 + (3a^2 - 2ax)]^3$.\nThe highest power of $x$ in $3a^2 - 2ax$ is 1, so $x^5$ can only come from ${}^3C_1(3x^2)^2(3a^2-2ax) = 27x^4(3a^2 - 2ax)$.\nThe $x^5$ part of this is $27x^4(-2ax) = -54ax^5$.\nOption (3) misses the factor 3 from ${}^3C_1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (number) with List-II (lower bound obtained from the first two terms of its binomial expansion written as $(1+x)^n$).\nList-I:\n(a) $(1.02)^{1000}$\n(b) $(1.002)^{5000}$\n(c) $(1.2)^{500}$\n(d) $(1.004)^{250000}$\nList-II:\n(i) 11\n(ii) 21\n(iii) 101\n(iv) 1001","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The lower bound is $1 + nx$ in each case.\n(a) $1 + 1000 \\times 0.02 = 21$, so (ii).\n(b) $1 + 5000 \\times 0.002 = 11$, so (i).\n(c) $1 + 500 \\times 0.2 = 101$, so (iii).\n(d) $1 + 250000 \\times 0.004 = 1001$, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $6^n - 5n$ leaves remainder 1 when divided by 25, for every positive integer n.\n(ii) In the expansion of $(x-2y)^5$, the coefficient of $x^2y^3$ is $-80$.\n(iii) The sum of the coefficients in the expansion of $(2x+3y)^5$ is 3125.\n(iv) The expansion of $\\left(x^2+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^4$ has 4 terms.\n(v) The coefficient of $x^3$ in $(x+2)^6$ is 160.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $6^n = (1+5)^n = 1 + 5n + 25k$, so $6^n - 5n = 25k + 1$.\n(ii) Correct: the coefficient is $-{}^5C_3\\cdot 2^3 = -10 \\times 8 = -80$.\n(iii) Correct: putting $x = y = 1$ gives $5^5 = 3125$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the index is 4, so there are $4+1 = 5$ terms.\n(v) Correct: the coefficient is ${}^6C_3\\cdot 2^3 = 20 \\times 8 = 160$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The coefficient of $x^2y^3$ in the expansion of $(x-2y)^5$ is $-80$.\nReason (R): In the expansion of $(x-2y)^5$, the coefficient of $x^2y^3$ is equal to the coefficient of $x^3y^2$, because ${}^5C_2 = {}^5C_3$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: for $x^2y^3$ we need $r=3$.\nCoefficient $= {}^5C_3(-2)^3 = 10 \\times (-8) = -80$. A is true.\nReason: for $x^3y^2$ we need $r=2$.\nCoefficient $= {}^5C_2(-2)^2 = 10 \\times 4 = 40$.\nSo the two coefficients are not equal. The binomial coefficients match, but the powers of $-2$ differ. R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $(2x+k)^4$, where $k \\neq 0$, the coefficient of $x^3$ equals the coefficient of $x^2$. The value of k is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{2}{3}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{3}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Pascal row 1 4 6 4 1. The power of 2x falls while the power of k rises.\nTerm with $x^3$: $4(2x)^3k = 32k\\,x^3$.\nTerm with $x^2$: $6(2x)^2k^2 = 24k^2\\,x^2$.\nSet the coefficients equal: $24k^2 = 32k$.\nSince $k \\neq 0$, divide by $8k$ to get $3k = 4$, so $k = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms in the expansion of $(x-2y)^5$ have a negative coefficient?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r x^{5-r}(-2)^r y^r$.\nThe sign is $(-1)^r$, so the coefficient is negative only when $r$ is odd.\nThe values of $r$ run from 0 to 5; the odd ones are $r = 1, 3, 5$.\nThese give the terms $-10x^4y$, $-80x^2y^3$ and $-32y^5$, which is 3 terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every positive integer $n$, $\\sum_{r=0}^{n} (-1)^r\\,2^r\\,{}^{n}C_r = 1$.\nReason (R): $(1-x)^n = {}^{n}C_0 - {}^{n}C_1x + {}^{n}C_2x^2 - \\dots + (-1)^n\\,{}^{n}C_nx^n$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"R: This is the special case $a = 1$, $b = -x$ of the binomial theorem, so R is true.\nA: Put $x = 2$ in R. The sum becomes $(1-2)^n = (-1)^n$.\nThis equals 1 only when $n$ is even. For $n = 1$ the sum is $1 - 2 = -1$.\nSo A is false and R is true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of $(2x + 1)^{7}$?","image":"","options":["560","280","1120","84"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $a = 2x$, $b = 1$, $n = 7$.\nGeneral term: ${}^{7}C_k (2x)^{7-k} (1)^k$.\nFor $x^4$ we need $7 - k = 4$, so $k = 3$.\nCoefficient = ${}^{7}C_3 \\cdot 2^4 = 35 \\times 16 = 560$.\nThe value 280 comes from wrongly using $2^3$ instead of $2^4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is certainly a factor of $17^{21} - 5^{21}$?","image":"","options":["12","11","13","22"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: $a - b$ divides $a^n - b^n$. Write $17^{21} = (12 + 5)^{21}$ and expand.\nEvery term except $5^{21}$ contains the factor 12, so $17^{21} - 5^{21}$ is a multiple of $17 - 5 = 12$.\nThe sum $a + b = 22$ divides $a^n - b^n$ only when n is even. Here n = 21 is odd, so 22 (and hence 11) is not guaranteed.\nModular checks show 11 and 13 do not divide it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) ${}^5C_0 + {}^5C_1 + \\dots + {}^5C_5$\n(Q) $\\sum_{r=0}^{4} 3^r\\,{}^4C_r$\n(R) ${}^6C_0 - {}^6C_1 + {}^6C_2 - \\dots + {}^6C_6$\n(S) Coefficient of $x^5$ in $(x+2)^6$\nList-II\n(1) 0\n(2) 12\n(3) 32\n(4) 256","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) The sum of all binomial coefficients is $2^5 = 32$, so P-3.\n(Q) This sum equals $(1+3)^4 = 4^4 = 256$, so Q-4.\n(R) The alternating sum comes from $(1-1)^6 = 0$, so R-1.\n(S) The $x^5$ term is ${}^6C_1x^5\\cdot 2 = 12x^5$, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Pascal's rule, ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$, is the key step in the induction proof of the binomial theorem. Using this rule, find the value of ${}^{10}C_4 + {}^{10}C_3$.","image":"","options":["330","210","165","462"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: ${}^{10}C_4 + {}^{10}C_3$.\nFormula: ${}^kC_r + {}^kC_{r-1} = {}^{k+1}C_r$, with $k = 10$ and $r = 4$.\nSubstituting: the sum is ${}^{11}C_4 = \\frac{11 \\times 10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{4!} = \\frac{7920}{24} = 330$.\nCheck directly: $210 + 120 = 330$.\nOption (2), 210, is only ${}^{10}C_4$. Option (3), 165, is ${}^{11}C_3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The expansion of $(2x+3y)^9$ has exactly 10 terms.\nStatement II: In every term of the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, the sum of the indices of $a$ and $b$ equals $n$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. $(a+b)^n$ has $n+1$ terms, so $(2x+3y)^9$ has $9+1 = 10$ terms.\nStatement II is true. The general term is ${}^nC_k a^{n-k}b^k$, and the sum of the indices is $(n-k)+k = n$ in every term.\nBoth statements are true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every positive integer $n$, ${}^nC_0-{}^nC_1+{}^nC_2-\\dots+(-1)^n\\,{}^nC_n=0$.\nReason (R): This result is obtained by putting $x=1$ in the expansion of $(1+x)^n$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: putting $x=1$ in $(1-x)^n={}^nC_0-{}^nC_1x+\\dots+(-1)^n{}^nC_nx^n$ gives $0^n=0$.\nR is false: putting $x=1$ in $(1+x)^n$ gives ${}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\dots+{}^nC_n=2^n$, not the alternating sum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the expansion of $(2x-3y)^3$, the coefficient of $x^2y$ is 36.\nStatement II: In every term of the expansion of $(2x-3y)^3$, the sum of the indices of x and y is 3.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Expand: $(2x)^3 + 3(2x)^2(-3y) + 3(2x)(-3y)^2 + (-3y)^3$.\nThis gives $8x^3 - 36x^2y + 54xy^2 - 27y^3$.\nStatement I is false: the coefficient of $x^2y$ is $-36$, not 36.\nStatement II is true: the index pairs are (3,0), (2,1), (1,2), (0,3), each adding to 3.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(1.01)^{1000000} > 10000$\nStatement II: $(1.1)^{10000} < 1000$\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. $(1+0.01)^{1000000} = 1 + 1000000(0.01) + \\text{(positive terms)} = 10001 + \\dots > 10000$.\nStatement II is false. $(1+0.1)^{10000} = 1 + 10000(0.1) + \\text{(positive terms)} = 1001 + \\dots > 1000$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Constant term in $(2x-3)^6$\n(B) Coefficient of $x$ in $(x+2)^6$\n(C) Coefficient of $xy^4$ in $(x-2y)^5$\n(D) Coefficient of $x^5$ in $(1-2x)^5$\nList-II\n(I) $-32$\n(II) 80\n(III) 192\n(IV) 729","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $r=6$: $(-3)^6 = 729$, so A matches (IV).\n(B) $r=5$: ${}^6C_5 \\cdot 2^5 = 6 \\times 32 = 192$, so B matches (III).\n(C) $r=4$: ${}^5C_4(-2)^4 = 5 \\times 16 = 80$, so C matches (II).\n(D) $r=5$: ${}^5C_5(-2)^5 = -32$, so D matches (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For the expansion of $(a+b)^{8}$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) Number of terms\n(Q) Sum of the indices of a and b in any term\n(R) Coefficient of $a^{5}b^{3}$\n(S) Coefficient of the 5th term\nList-II:\n(1) 70\n(2) 9\n(3) 56\n(4) 8","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) The number of terms is $8 + 1 = 9$, so P matches (2).\n(Q) The indices always add up to $n = 8$, so Q matches (4).\n(R) For $a^5b^3$, $k = 3$, so the coefficient is ${}^{8}C_3 = 56$. R matches (3).\n(S) The 5th term has $k = 4$, so the coefficient is ${}^{8}C_4 = 70$. S matches (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}3^r\\,{}^nC_r=4096$, then the value of $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}(-3)^r\\,{}^nC_r$ is","image":"","options":["$-64$","$0$","$64$","$729$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Putting $x=3$ in $(1+x)^n$ gives $\\sum 3^r\\,{}^nC_r = 4^n$.\nSo $4^n = 4096 = 4^6$, which gives $n=6$.\nPutting $x=-3$ gives $\\sum (-3)^r\\,{}^nC_r = (1-3)^n = (-2)^6 = 64$.\nThe answer is positive because the index 6 is even, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The sum of all the coefficients in the expansion of $(2x-3y)^5$ is","image":"","options":["$-1$","$1$","$32$","$-243$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each term of the expansion has the form (coefficient)$\\times x^{5-r}y^r$.\nPutting $x = y = 1$ makes every power equal to 1, so the expression reduces to the sum of all the coefficients.\nSum $= (2\\cdot1-3\\cdot1)^5 = (-1)^5 = -1$.\nOption (2) is wrong because it ignores the sign of the odd power.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"When $\\left(1 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^4$, $x \\neq 0$, is fully expanded and like terms are combined, the number of distinct terms is:","image":"","options":["15","9","5","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A general term contains $x^{j-k}$ with $j + k \\le 4$, $j, k \\ge 0$.\nSo $j - k$ can take every integer value from $-4$ to $4$, which gives 9 possible powers.\nThe 15 combinations of $(i,j,k)$ collapse into these 9 powers, because $\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{2}{x}\\right) = -1$.\nEach combined coefficient is non-zero: $\\frac{1}{16}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, -4, -5, 16, 8, -32, 16$.\nHence there are 9 terms, not 15.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{k}{x}\\right)^6$, $x \\neq 0$, the term independent of $x$ is 240 and the coefficient of $x^3$ is negative. The value of $k$ is","image":"","options":["-2","2","4","-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r k^r x^{12-3r}$.\nTerm independent of $x$: $12-3r=0$ gives $r=4$, so $15k^4 = 240$. Then $k^4 = 16$ and $k = \\pm 2$.\nCoefficient of $x^3$: $12-3r=3$ gives $r=3$, so the coefficient is $20k^3$.\nThis must be negative, so $k^3 < 0$, which means $k = -2$.\nCheck: $20(-2)^3 = -160 < 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"After complete simplification, how many terms are there in the expansion of $\\left[(a+2b)^2(a-2b)^2\\right]^2$?","image":"","options":["4","5","8","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First combine the brackets: $(a+2b)^2(a-2b)^2 = [(a+2b)(a-2b)]^2 = (a^2-4b^2)^2$.\nSo the whole expression is $(a^2-4b^2)^4$.\nTreat it as a binomial with first term $a^2$, second term $-4b^2$, and index 4.\nIt has 4 + 1 = 5 terms: $a^8, a^6b^2, a^4b^4, a^2b^6, b^8$.\nNone of these cancel, because each has a distinct power.\n9 would come from counting every power of a from 8 down to 0, but odd powers never appear.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $(1-2x)^5(x+2)^6$ is","image":"","options":["320","$-320$","160","5440"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Coefficients of $(1-2x)^5$ for $x^0, x^1, x^2, x^3$: 1, $-10$, 40, $-80$.\nCoefficients of $(x+2)^6$ for $x^0, x^1, x^2, x^3$: 64, 192, 240, 160.\nAn $x^3$ term comes from pairs of powers that add to 3:\n$1(160) + (-10)(240) + 40(192) + (-80)(64)$.\n$= 160 - 2400 + 7680 - 5120 = 320$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the expansion of $(a+b)^4$ written in descending powers of a. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) It has 5 terms.\n(ii) The index of a in the second term is 3.\n(iii) The index of b in the fourth term is 4.\n(iv) The sum of the indices of a and b is 4 in every term.\n(v) Coefficients equidistant from the two ends are equal.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: there are 4 + 1 = 5 terms.\n(ii) Correct: the second term is $4a^3b$, so the index of a is 3.\n(iii) Incorrect: the fourth term is $4ab^3$, so the index of b is 3, not 4.\n(iv) Correct: every term has the form $a^{4-r}b^r$, and the indices add to 4.\n(v) Correct: the coefficients 1, 4, 6, 4, 1 are symmetric.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Pascal's triangle is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Every row begins and ends with 1","Each interior entry is the sum of the two entries just above it, which is the identity ${}^nC_r+{}^nC_{r-1}={}^{n+1}C_r$","A similar arrangement was given by Pingla and is called Meru Prastara","The row for index $n$ contains exactly $n$ entries"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: ${}^nC_0={}^nC_n=1$.\n(2) Correct: this is how each new row is built, and the same identity is used in the induction proof.\n(3) Correct: NCERT notes that Pingla called this arrangement Meru Prastara.\n(4) Incorrect: the row for index $n$ runs from ${}^nC_0$ to ${}^nC_n$, which is $n+1$ entries.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For $x \\geq 1$, $(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1})^3 + (x-\\sqrt{x^2-1})^3$ simplifies to","image":"","options":["$8x^3 - 6x$","$2x^3 + 6x$","$8x^3 + 6x$","$4x^3 - 3x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $s = \\sqrt{x^2-1}$.\nThen $(x+s)^3 + (x-s)^3 = 2(x^3 + 3xs^2)$, because the odd powers of s cancel.\nSubstitute $s^2 = x^2 - 1$: $2[x^3 + 3x(x^2-1)] = 2(4x^3 - 3x)$.\nThis gives $8x^3 - 6x$. Option (4) misses the factor 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(1.1)^{10000} > 1000$.\nStatement II: In the expansion of $(1+0.1)^{10000}$ all terms are positive, and the first two terms alone add up to 1001.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand: $(1+0.1)^{10000} = 1 + 10000(0.1) + \\text{(positive terms)}$.\nThe first two terms give $1 + 1000 = 1001$, so Statement II is true.\nAll the remaining terms are positive, so $(1.1)^{10000} > 1001 > 1000$.\nHence Statement I is also true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The third term in the expansion of $(a+b)^4$ is","image":"","options":["$4a^3b$","$4ab^3$","$6a^2b^2$","$6a^3b^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Powers of a go 4, 3, 2, 1, 0 and powers of b go 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.\nSo the third term contains $a^2b^2$.\nThe Pascal row for index 4 is 1 4 6 4 1, so the third coefficient is 6.\nThird term = $6a^2b^2$.\n$6a^3b^2$ fails because its indices add to 5, not 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the sum of all the entries in the row for index 6 of Pascal's triangle?","image":"","options":["32","64","128","36"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: put $x = 1$ in $(1+x)^n$ to get $^nC_0 + {}^nC_1 + \\dots + {}^nC_n = 2^n$.\nFor $n = 6$ the sum is $2^6 = 64$.\nCheck by adding the row: 1+6+15+20+15+6+1 = 64.\nThe value 32 is the sum for index 5, and 128 is the sum for index 7.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion $(a+b)^{n} = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} {}^{n}C_k\\, a^{n-k} b^{k}$ with $n = 10$, which of the following is the term containing $b^{4}$?","image":"","options":["${}^{10}C_4\\, a^{6} b^{4}$","${}^{10}C_4\\, a^{4} b^{4}$","${}^{10}C_5\\, a^{6} b^{4}$","${}^{10}C_6\\, a^{4} b^{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the sigma form, the power of $b$ equals $k$. So $b^4$ corresponds to $k = 4$.\nThe power of $a$ is $n - k = 10 - 4 = 6$.\nThe coefficient is ${}^{10}C_4$, so the term is ${}^{10}C_4 a^6 b^4$.\nOption (2) fails because the indices add to 8, not 10. In every term the indices of $a$ and $b$ must add up to $n$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"By writing $1 + x - 2x^2$ as $(1 + x) - 2x^2$, the coefficient of $x^4$ in the expansion of $(1 + x - 2x^2)^6$ is found to be:","image":"","options":["$-45$","$15$","$45$","$-105$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand $[(1+x) - 2x^2]^6 = \\sum_k {}^6C_k(-2x^2)^k(1+x)^{6-k}$.\nFor $x^4$ we need $x^{4-2k}$ from $(1+x)^{6-k}$:\n$k=0$: ${}^6C_4 = 15$.\n$k=1$: $6(-2)\\,{}^5C_2 = -120$.\n$k=2$: $15(4)\\,{}^4C_0 = 60$.\nSum: $15 - 120 + 60 = -45$.\nCheck: $1 + x - 2x^2 = (1+2x)(1-x)$, and expanding $(1+2x)^6(1-x)^6$ also gives $-45$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Coefficient of $x^4$ in $(x+2)^6$\n(B) Coefficient of $x^3y^2$ in $(2x+3y)^5$\n(C) Coefficient of $x^2y^3$ in $(x-2y)^5$\n(D) Sum of all the coefficients in $(1+x)^7$\nList-II\n(I) 128\n(II) $-80$\n(III) 60\n(IV) 720","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Coefficient $= {}^6C_2 \\cdot 2^2 = 15 \\times 4 = 60$, which is (III).\n(B) Coefficient $= {}^5C_2 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 10 \\times 8 \\times 9 = 720$, which is (IV).\n(C) Coefficient $= {}^5C_3(-2)^3 = 10 \\times (-8) = -80$, which is (II).\n(D) Put $x=1$: sum $= 2^7 = 128$, which is (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"While computing $(98)^5$ as $(100-2)^5$, what is the value of the third term of the expansion?","image":"","options":["40000000","4000000","20000000","−40000000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: the third term is ${}^5C_2 (100)^{3}(-2)^2$.\nSubstituting: ${}^5C_2 = 10$, $(100)^3 = 10^6$ and $(-2)^2 = 4$.\nSo the term is $10 \\times 10^6 \\times 4 = 40000000$.\nIt is positive because $-2$ is raised to an even power.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following expressions are divisible by 8 for every positive integer n?\n(i) $9^n - 1$\n(ii) $9^n + 7$\n(iii) $5^n - 1$\n(iv) $17^n - 9^n$\n(v) $13^n - 5^n$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: $9^n = (8+1)^n = 8k + 1$, so $9^n - 1 = 8k$.\n(ii) Yes: $9^n + 7 = (9^n - 1) + 8$.\n(iii) No: for n = 1 the value is 4.\n(iv) Yes: $17 - 9 = 8$, and $a - b$ divides $a^n - b^n$.\n(v) Yes: $13 - 5 = 8$.\nSo 4 expressions qualify.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The 4th term in the expansion of $(x-2y)^5$ is","image":"","options":["$-80x^2y^3$","$80x^2y^3$","$-40x^3y^2$","$80xy^4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r\\, x^{5-r}(-2y)^r$.\nThe 4th term has $r+1=4$, so $r=3$.\n$T_4 = {}^5C_3\\, x^2 (-2y)^3 = 10 \\times x^2 \\times (-8y^3) = -80x^2y^3$.\nThe sign is negative because $r$ is odd, so $80x^2y^3$ is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $\\sum_{r=0}^{3} 3^r\\,{}^{3}C_r$ is","image":"","options":["27","64","81","48"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the sum $\\sum_{r=0}^{3} {}^{3}C_r\\,3^r$.\nFormula: $(1+x)^n = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} {}^{n}C_r x^r$. Put $x = 3$ to get $\\sum {}^{n}C_r 3^r = 4^n$.\nSubstituting $n = 3$: the sum is $4^3 = 64$.\nCheck term by term: $1 + 3(3) + 3(9) + 1(27) = 1 + 9 + 27 + 27 = 64$.\nThe option 27 comes from using $3^3$ instead of $(1+3)^3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match each number in List-I with its approximation in List-II, where each approximation uses only the first three terms of the binomial expansion.\nList-I\n(A) $(0.98)^5$\n(B) $(1.01)^8$\n(C) $(0.97)^4$\n(D) $(1.03)^5$\nList-II\n(I) 1.159\n(II) 0.8854\n(III) 0.904\n(IV) 1.0828","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $1 - 5(0.02) + 10(0.0004) = 0.904$, which is III.\n(B) $1 + 8(0.01) + 28(0.0001) = 1.0828$, which is IV.\n(C) $1 - 4(0.03) + 6(0.0009) = 0.8854$, which is II.\n(D) $1 + 5(0.03) + 10(0.0009) = 1.159$, which is I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For every positive integer $n$, what is the remainder when $8^n - 7n$ is divided by $49$?","image":"","options":["0","1","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $8^n = (1+7)^n = {}^nC_0 + {}^nC_1\\,7 + {}^nC_2\\,7^2 + \\dots + 7^n$.\nSo $8^n = 1 + 7n + 49\\,({}^nC_2 + 7\\,{}^nC_3 + \\dots + 7^{n-2})$.\nHence $8^n - 7n = 49k + 1$, where $k$ is a whole number.\nCheck with $n = 2$: $64 - 14 = 50 = 49 + 1$.\nThe remainder is therefore 1. It is not 0, because the constant term $1$ is left over after the multiple of $49$ is removed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the coefficient of $x^{5}$ in the expansion of $\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{3}{x}\\right)^{7}$, $x \\neq 0$?","image":"","options":["945","2835","189","315"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $a = x^2$, $b = 3x^{-1}$, $n = 7$.\nGeneral term: ${}^{7}C_k (x^2)^{7-k} 3^k x^{-k} = {}^{7}C_k 3^k x^{14-3k}$.\nSetting $14 - 3k = 5$ gives $k = 3$.\nCoefficient = ${}^{7}C_3 \\cdot 3^3 = 35 \\times 27 = 945$.\nThe option 2835 comes from wrongly using $k = 4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $^{10}C_0-{}^{10}C_1+{}^{10}C_2-\\dots+{}^{10}C_{10}=0$\nReason (R): $(1-x)^n={}^nC_0-{}^nC_1x+{}^nC_2x^2-\\dots+(-1)^n\\,{}^nC_nx^n$, and putting $x=1$ makes the left side zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it is the special case $a=1$, $b=-x$ of the binomial theorem.\nPutting $n=10$ and $x=1$ gives $(1-1)^{10}=0$ on the left side.\nThe right side becomes exactly the alternating sum in A, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the binomial theorem, find the remainder when $6^{12}$ is divided by 25.","image":"","options":["1","11","6","21"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $6^{12} = (1+5)^{12} = 1 + 12(5) + {}^{12}C_2\\,5^2 + {}^{12}C_3\\,5^3 + \\dots$\nEvery term from the third onward contains $5^2 = 25$, so $6^{12} = 25k + 1 + 60 = 25k + 61$.\nSince $61 = 2 \\times 25 + 11$, we get $6^{12} = 25(k+2) + 11$.\nSo the remainder is 11.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The sum of all the coefficients in the expansion of $(1-x)^7$ is 0.\nStatement II: The sum of the absolute values of the coefficients in the expansion of $(1-x)^7$ is 128.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: putting $x=1$ gives $(1-1)^7=0$. True.\nStatement II: the coefficients are $(-1)^r\\,{}^7C_r$, so their absolute values are $^7C_r$.\nTheir sum is $\\sum {}^7C_r = 2^7 = 128$. This equals the value of $(1+x)^7$ at $x=1$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The sum of all the numerical coefficients in the expansion of $\\left(1 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{2}{x}\\right)^4$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{16}$","$\\frac{81}{16}$","$1$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The sum of the coefficients is the value of the expression at $x = 1$.\nAt $x = 1$: $1 + \\frac{1}{2} - 2 = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\n$\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{16}$.\nCheck with the full expansion: $\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} - 4 - 5 + 16 + 8 - 32 + 16 = \\frac{1}{16}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every positive integer n, ${}^nC_0 - {}^nC_1 + {}^nC_2 - \\dots + (-1)^n\\,{}^nC_n = 0$.\nReason (R): This result is obtained by putting $x = 1$ in the expansion of $(1+x)^n$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: Put $x = 1$ in $(1-x)^n = {}^nC_0 - {}^nC_1x + {}^nC_2x^2 - \\dots + (-1)^n\\,{}^nC_nx^n$.\nThe left side becomes $0^n = 0$, so the alternating sum is 0. A is true.\nR: Putting $x = 1$ in $(1+x)^n$ gives ${}^nC_0 + {}^nC_1 + \\dots + {}^nC_n = 2^n$. That is the sum with all plus signs, not the alternating sum. R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(0.98)^6$ is approximated using only the first three terms of the expansion of $(1-0.02)^6$. By how much does this approximation exceed the exact value of $(0.98)^6$?","image":"","options":["0.000157619136","0.000160000000","0.000162380864","0.000002400000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Three-term approximation: $1 - 6(0.02) + 15(0.0004) = 0.886$.\nThe dropped terms are $-20(0.02)^3 + 15(0.02)^4 - 6(0.02)^5 + (0.02)^6$.\nIn numbers: $-0.00016 + 0.0000024 - 0.0000000192 + 0.000000000064 = -0.000157619136$.\nSo the exact value is $0.886 - 0.000157619136 = 0.885842380864$.\nThe excess is therefore $0.000157619136$.\nOption (2) wrongly keeps only the fourth term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $(1-2x)^5$, the coefficient of $x^2$ is","image":"","options":["40","-40","20","-80"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r(-2x)^r$.\nFor $x^2$, take $r=2$.\nCoefficient $= {}^5C_2(-2)^2 = 10 \\times 4 = 40$.\nThe value is positive because $(-2)^2 > 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of ${}^{10}C_0 + {}^{10}C_1 + {}^{10}C_2 + \\ldots + {}^{10}C_{10}$ is","image":"","options":["1000","1024","512","2048"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Start from $(1+x)^n = {}^nC_0 + {}^nC_1x + \\ldots + {}^nC_nx^n$.\nPut $x = 1$ to get $2^n = \\sum {}^nC_r$.\nWith $n = 10$, the sum is $2^{10} = 1024$.\nThe option 512 would correspond to $2^9$, which is the wrong index.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the result proved in NCERT with the binomial theorem, what is the remainder when $6^{10} - 50$ is divided by 25?","image":"","options":["0","1","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $6^n - 5n$ with $n = 10$, so $5n = 50$.\nFormula: $6^n = (1+5)^n = 1 + 5n + 5^2(^nC_2 + 5\\,^nC_3 + \\dots)$\nSo $6^n - 5n = 25k + 1$ for every positive integer n.\nSubstituting n = 10: $6^{10} - 50 = 25k + 1$.\nSo the remainder is 1 (check: $6^{10} = 60466176$ and $60466126 = 25 \\times 2418645 + 1$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The coefficient of $x^4$ in the expansion of $(2x-3)^6$ is 2160.\nStatement II: The sum of all the coefficients in the expansion of $(2x-3)^6$ is $-1$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r (2x)^{6-r}(-3)^r$. For $x^4$ we need $r=2$.\nCoefficient $= 15 \\times 16 \\times 9 = 2160$. True.\nStatement II: the sum of the coefficients is the value of the expression at $x=1$.\n$(2-3)^6 = (-1)^6 = 1$, not $-1$. False.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the coefficient of $x^2$ in the expansion of $(x-2)^6$?","image":"","options":["240","$-240$","60","160"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r\\, x^{6-r}(-2)^r$.\nFor $x^2$ we need $6-r=2$, so $r=4$.\nCoefficient $= {}^6C_4(-2)^4 = 15 \\times 16 = 240$.\nThe sign is positive because $(-2)^4$ has an even power. This rules out $-240$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"After expanding and simplifying, how many terms are there in $(x+2)^7 + (x-2)^7$?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each expansion has 8 terms, $^7C_k x^{7-k} (\\pm 2)^k$.\nWhen the two expansions are added, terms with odd k (odd powers of 2) cancel. Terms with even k double.\nThe surviving values are k = 0, 2, 4, 6. These give the terms in $x^7, x^5, x^3, x$.\nSo there are 4 terms. The tempting answer 8 ignores the cancellation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using the Pascal's triangle row 1 5 10 10 5 1, the coefficient of $x^2y^3$ in the expansion of $(2x - 3y)^5$ is:","image":"","options":["$-1080$","$1080$","$-720$","$720$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The term containing $x^2y^3$ is the fourth term of the expansion. Its Pascal coefficient is 10, so the term is $10(2x)^2(-3y)^3$.\nWork out the powers: $(2x)^2 = 4x^2$ and $(-3y)^3 = -27y^3$.\nMultiply: $10 \\times 4 \\times (-27) = -1080$.\nOption (2), 1080, is the coefficient for $(2x+3y)^5$; it ignores the odd power of $-3y$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(\\sqrt{2}+1)^5 - (\\sqrt{2}-1)^5 = 82$.\nStatement II: $(\\sqrt{2}+1)^5 + (\\sqrt{2}-1)^5$ is an integer.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Take $a=\\sqrt2$, $b=1$.\nStatement I: the difference keeps the odd powers of $b$, so it equals $2[5a^4b+10a^2b^3+b^5]=2[5(4)+10(2)+1]=2(41)=82$. True.\nStatement II: the sum keeps the even powers of $b$, so it equals $2[a^5+10a^3b^2+5ab^4]=2[4\\sqrt2+20\\sqrt2+5\\sqrt2]=58\\sqrt2$. This is irrational, so Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(1.02)^{100} > 3$.\nReason (R): For $x > 0$ and any integer $n \\geq 2$, $(1+x)^n > 1 + nx$, because every remaining term of the binomial expansion is positive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + {}^nC_2x^2 + \\dots$ and every term after $nx$ is positive when $x > 0$.\nA is true: $(1.02)^{100} = 1 + 100(0.02) + \\text{positive terms} = 3 + \\text{positive terms} > 3$.\nA follows directly from R with $x = 0.02$ and $n = 100$, so R explains A. This is the same idea as NCERT's comparison of $(1.01)^{1000000}$ with 10000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the sigma form of the binomial theorem, $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} {}^nC_k\\, a^{n-k} b^k$, which term is obtained for $n = 8$ and $k = 3$?","image":"","options":["${}^8C_3\\, a^5 b^3$","${}^8C_3\\, a^3 b^5$","${}^8C_4\\, a^5 b^3$","${}^8C_3\\, a^4 b^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The general summand is ${}^nC_k\\, a^{n-k} b^k$.\nPut $n = 8$ and $k = 3$. This gives ${}^8C_3\\, a^{8-3} b^3 = {}^8C_3\\, a^5 b^3$. It is the 4th term, since k starts at 0.\nCheck: the indices add to $5 + 3 = 8$, which equals the index of the binomial.\nOption (2) swaps the powers. Option (3) uses the wrong coefficient. In option (4) the indices add to 7, not 8.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9$ is divisible by 128 for every positive integer n.\nReason (R): $9^{n+1} = (1+8)^{n+1} = 1 + 8(n+1) + 64\\left[{}^{n+1}C_2 + 8\\,{}^{n+1}C_3 + \\dots\\right]$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R: True. Binomial expansion gives $9^{n+1} = 1 + 8(n+1) + 8^2\\,{}^{n+1}C_2 + 8^3\\,{}^{n+1}C_3 + \\dots$\nSo $9^{n+1} - 8n - 9 = 64[{}^{n+1}C_2 + 8\\,{}^{n+1}C_3 + \\dots]$, which proves divisibility by 64 only.\nA: False. For n = 1, $81 - 8 - 9 = 64$, which is not divisible by 128.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^8$, $x \\neq 0$, is","image":"","options":["$-70$","$56$","$70$","$28$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^8C_r\\, x^{8-r}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^r = {}^8C_r (-1)^r x^{8-2r}$.\nFor the term free of $x$, set $8 - 2r = 0$, so $r = 4$. This is the 5th term, which is also the middle term.\nSo $T_5 = {}^8C_4 (-1)^4 = 70 \\times 1 = 70$.\nThe sign is positive because $(-1)^4 = 1$, so $-70$ is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For $n = 10$, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\sum_{r=0}^{10} {}^{10}C_r = 1024$\n(ii) $\\sum_{r=0}^{10} (-1)^r\\,{}^{10}C_r = 0$\n(iii) $\\sum_{r=0}^{10} 2^r\\,{}^{10}C_r = 3^{10}$\n(iv) ${}^{10}C_0 + {}^{10}C_2 + {}^{10}C_4 + \\dots + {}^{10}C_{10} = 512$\n(v) The expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ has 10 terms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: put $x=1$ in $(1+x)^{10}$ to get $2^{10} = 1024$.\n(ii) Correct: put $x=1$ in $(1-x)^{10}$ to get $0$.\n(iii) Correct: put $x=2$ in $(1+x)^{10}$ to get $3^{10}$.\n(iv) Correct: add (i) and (ii). This gives $2(\\text{sum of even-}r\\text{ terms}) = 1024$, so the even sum is $512$.\n(v) Incorrect: the expansion has $10+1 = 11$ terms.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $7^{40} - 1$ is divisible by 50.\nReason (R): For every positive integer $n$, $7^n - 1$ is divisible by 50.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: $7^2 = 49 = 50 - 1$, so $7^{40} = (50-1)^{20}$.\nExpanding: $(50-1)^{20} = 50^{20} - {}^{20}C_1\\,50^{19} + \\dots - {}^{20}C_{19}\\,50 + (-1)^{20}$.\nEvery term except the last, $(-1)^{20} = 1$, contains the factor 50. So $7^{40} - 1 = 50m$, and A is true.\nR is false. For $n = 1$, $7 - 1 = 6$, which is not divisible by 50.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms in the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{3}{x}\\right)^{10}$ contain a positive power of $x$?","image":"","options":["6","7","8","11"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^{10}C_r\\, 3^r\\, x^{20-3r}$, with $0 \\le r \\le 10$.\nFor a positive power: $20 - 3r > 0$, so $r < \\frac{20}{3}$, giving $r = 0, 1, \\dots, 6$.\nThat is 7 terms.\nSince $20 - 3r = 0$ has no integer solution, there is no constant term to worry about.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many terms in the expansion of $\\left(x^2+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^4$ contain a positive power of $x$?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: ${}^4C_k (x^2)^{4-k}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^k = {}^4C_k\\,3^k\\,x^{8-3k}$, for $k = 0,1,2,3,4$.\nPowers of $x$: $8, 5, 2, -1, -4$.\nThe positive powers are $8$, $5$ and $2$, so there are 3 such terms: $x^8$, $12x^5$ and $54x^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The simplified value of $(x+1)^4 + (x-1)^4$ is","image":"","options":["$2x^4 + 6x^2 + 2$","$2x^4 + 12x^2 + 2$","$2x^4 + 12x^2$","$x^4 + 6x^2 + 1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$(x\\pm1)^4 = x^4 \\pm 4x^3 + 6x^2 \\pm 4x + 1$.\nOn adding, the odd-power terms $\\pm 4x^3$ and $\\pm 4x$ cancel.\nThe even-power terms double: $2(x^4 + 6x^2 + 1)$.\nThis equals $2x^4 + 12x^2 + 2$. Option (1) forgets to double the middle term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the coefficient of $x^3$ in $(1+x)^3 + (1+x)^4 + (1+x)^5 + \\dots + (1+x)^9$?","image":"","options":["210","120","252","330"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: In $(1+x)^n = \\sum {}^nC_k x^k$, the coefficient of $x^3$ is ${}^nC_3$. So the required sum is $S = {}^3C_3 + {}^4C_3 + \\dots + {}^9C_3$.\nStep 2: Replace ${}^3C_3$ by ${}^4C_4$, since both equal 1.\nStep 3: Apply Pascal's rule repeatedly. ${}^4C_4 + {}^4C_3 = {}^5C_4$. Then ${}^5C_4 + {}^5C_3 = {}^6C_4$, and so on, up to ${}^9C_4 + {}^9C_3 = {}^{10}C_4$.\nStep 4: ${}^{10}C_4 = 210$.\nCheck: $1 + 4 + 10 + 20 + 35 + 56 + 84 = 210$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"What is the remainder when $7^{103} + 6^{25}$ is divided by $25$?","image":"","options":["1","18","19","24"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First term: $7^{103} = 7\\,(7^2)^{51} = 7\\,(50-1)^{51}$.\nExpanding, every term of $(50-1)^{51}$ except $(-1)^{51}$ contains the factor $50$, so it is a multiple of $25$. Hence $7^{103} = 25p - 7$.\nSecond term: $6^{25} = (1+5)^{25} = 1 + 25\\cdot5 + 25\\,(\\dots)$, so $6^{25} = 25q + 1$.\nAdding: $7^{103} + 6^{25} = 25(p+q) - 6 = 25(p+q-1) + 19$.\nSo the remainder is 19. Taking $7^{103}$ alone gives 18, which is the tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the history and structure of Pascal's triangle is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The row for index 6 is 1 6 15 20 15 6 1","Pingla described the arrangement as Meru-Prastara in his book Chhanda shastra","The term 'binomial coefficients' was first introduced by Blaise Pascal","Each row begins and ends with 1, and every inner entry is the sum of the two entries just above it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: ${}^6C_0, \\ldots, {}^6C_6$ are 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1.\n(2) Correct: NCERT credits Pingla's Chhanda shastra (200 B.C.) with the Meru-Prastara.\n(3) Incorrect: the term 'binomial coefficients' was introduced by the German mathematician Michael Stipel, around 1544.\n(4) Correct: this follows from ${}^nC_r + {}^nC_{r-1} = {}^{n+1}C_r$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\left(x^2+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^4$ has no term independent of $x$.\n(ii) The middle term of $(x+2)^6$ is $160x^3$.\n(iii) The sum of the coefficients in $(2x+3y)^5$ is $3125$.\n(iv) The sum of the coefficients in $(x-2y)^5$ is $1$.\n(v) The greatest binomial coefficient in $(a+b)^7$ is 35, and it occurs twice.","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the general term has power $x^{8-3r}$, and $8-3r=0$ has no integer solution.\n(ii) Correct: there are 7 terms, so the middle term is the 4th, ${}^6C_3x^3\\cdot2^3=160x^3$.\n(iii) Correct: putting $x=y=1$ gives $5^5=3125$.\n(iv) Incorrect: putting $x=y=1$ gives $(1-2)^5=-1$.\n(v) Correct: ${}^7C_3={}^7C_4=35$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"For the expansion of $\\left(2x^2 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^6$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Number of terms\n(B) Term independent of $x$\n(C) Coefficient of $x^6$\n(D) Coefficient of the middle term\nList-II:\n(i) 240\n(ii) $-160$\n(iii) 7\n(iv) 60","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r\\, 2^{6-r}(-1)^r x^{12-3r}$.\n(A) There are $6 + 1 = 7$ terms, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Constant term: $12 - 3r = 0$ gives $r = 4$, and $15 \\cdot 4 = 60$, so B matches (iv).\n(C) For $x^6$: $r = 2$, and $15 \\cdot 16 = 240$, so C matches (i).\n(D) Middle term is $T_4$ ($r = 3$): $20 \\cdot 8 \\cdot (-1) = -160$, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"When $(x+a)^7 - (x-a)^7$ is expanded and simplified, how many non-zero terms remain?","image":"","options":["3","4","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $(x+a)^7=\\sum_{r=0}^{7} {}^7C_r x^{7-r}a^r$ and $(x-a)^7=\\sum_{r=0}^{7} (-1)^r\\, {}^7C_r x^{7-r}a^r$.\nOn subtracting, terms with even $r$ cancel. Terms with odd $r$ double.\nThe surviving values are $r=1,3,5,7$, which gives $2(7x^6a+35x^4a^3+21x^2a^5+a^7)$.\nSo 4 terms remain. Option 8 is tempting because it counts all terms of the expansion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3})^4 - (\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{3})^4$ is","image":"","options":["$32\\sqrt{15}$","$64\\sqrt{15}$","$128\\sqrt{15}$","$16\\sqrt{15}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: $(a+b)^4 - (a-b)^4 = 2(4a^3b + 4ab^3) = 8ab(a^2 + b^2)$.\nHere $a = \\sqrt5$, $b = \\sqrt3$, so $ab = \\sqrt{15}$ and $a^2 + b^2 = 8$.\nValue $= 8\\times\\sqrt{15}\\times8 = 64\\sqrt{15}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Using only the first three terms of the binomial expansion, an approximate value of $(1.02)^6$ is:","image":"","options":["1.126","1.120","1.106","1.240"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $(1.02)^6 = (1 + 0.02)^6$.\nFormula: $(1+x)^n \\approx {}^nC_0 + {}^nC_1x + {}^nC_2x^2$\nSubstituting: $1 + 6(0.02) + 15(0.02)^2 = 1 + 0.12 + 15(0.0004) = 1 + 0.12 + 0.006$\nSo $(1.02)^6 \\approx 1.126$. The option 1.120 keeps only two terms, and 1.240 wrongly uses $x = 0.04$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{3}{x}\\right)^4$, the coefficient of $x^2$ is","image":"","options":["12","54","108","36"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^4C_r (x^2)^{4-r}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^r = {}^4C_r\\, 3^r\\, x^{8-3r}$.\nFor $x^2$: $8 - 3r = 2$, so $r = 2$.\nCoefficient $= {}^4C_2 \\cdot 3^2 = 6 \\times 9 = 54$.\nThe value 108 is the coefficient of $x^{-1}$ (when $r = 3$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"In Pascal's triangle, the row for index 3 is 1 3 3 1. Which of the following is the row for index 4?","image":"","options":["1 3 6 3 1","1 4 4 4 1","1 5 10 10 5 1","1 4 6 4 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each row begins and ends with 1.\nThe inner entries are sums of adjacent entries in the row above: 1+3 = 4, 3+3 = 6, 3+1 = 4.\nSo the next row is 1 4 6 4 1.\n1 5 10 10 5 1 is the row for index 5, not 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $\\frac{1}{x}$ in the expansion of $\\left(\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^5$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{10}{9}$","$\\frac{10}{27}$","$\\frac{5}{9}$","$\\frac{10}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^{5-r}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^r = {}^5C_r\\, 3^{-(5-r)}\\, x^{5-2r}$.\nFor $x^{-1}$: $5 - 2r = -1$, so $r = 3$.\nCoefficient $= {}^5C_3 \\cdot 3^{-2} = \\frac{10}{9}$.\nThe value $\\frac{10}{27}$ is the coefficient of $x$ (when $r = 2$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^5$ in the expansion of $\\left(x^2 + \\frac{3}{x}\\right)^7$, $x \\neq 0$, is","image":"","options":["$315$","$945$","$2835$","$105$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^7C_r (x^2)^{7-r}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)^r = {}^7C_r\\, 3^r x^{14-3r}$.\nFor $x^5$, set $14 - 3r = 5$, so $r = 3$.\nCoefficient $= {}^7C_3 \\times 3^3 = 35 \\times 27 = 945$.\n$315$ uses $3^2$ instead of $3^3$, and $2835$ uses $3^4$, so both are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II (n is a positive integer):\nList-I\n(a) $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}{}^nC_r$\n(b) $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}(-1)^r\\,{}^nC_r$\n(c) $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}3^r\\,{}^nC_r$\n(d) $\\sum_{r=0}^{n}2^r\\,{}^nC_r$\nList-II\n(i) 0\n(ii) $3^n$\n(iii) $2^n$\n(iv) $4^n$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each sum is $(1+x)^n=\\sum {}^nC_r x^r$ evaluated at a particular $x$.\n(a) $x=1$ gives $2^n$, so (iii).\n(b) $x=-1$ gives $0$, so (i).\n(c) $x=3$ gives $4^n$, so (iv).\n(d) $x=2$ gives $3^n$, so (ii).\nOption (2) swaps (c) and (d) by confusing $x$ with $1+x$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the expansion of $(a+b)^n$, where n is a positive integer, the first term is $a^n$ and the last term is $b^n$, each with coefficient 1.\nReason (R): ${}^{n}C_0 = {}^{n}C_n = 1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The first term is ${}^{n}C_0 a^n b^0$ and the last term is ${}^{n}C_n a^0 b^n$.\nR is true: ${}^{n}C_0 = \\frac{n!}{0!\\,n!} = 1$ and ${}^{n}C_n = \\frac{n!}{n!\\,0!} = 1$.\nThese values of the coefficients make the first and last terms exactly $a^n$ and $b^n$, so A is true.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"When $(x-2y)^5$ is expanded fully, how many of its terms have a negative coefficient?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^5C_r x^{5-r}(-2y)^r$.\nThe sign is $(-1)^r$, so the term is negative only when $r$ is odd.\nWith $r = 0$ to $5$, the odd values are $r = 1, 3, 5$.\nThese give the terms $-10x^4y$, $-80x^2y^3$ and $-32y^5$.\nSo 3 terms are negative.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The term independent of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(2x^2 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^9$ is $-672$.\nStatement II: The expansion of $\\left(2x - \\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^9$ has two middle terms, and their product is independent of $x$ and equals $-15876$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: $T_{r+1} = {}^9C_r\\, 2^{9-r}(-1)^r x^{18-3r}$. Setting $18 - 3r = 0$ gives $r = 6$, so the term is $84 \\times 2^3 \\times (+1) = 672$, not $-672$. Incorrect.\nStatement II: The index 9 is odd, so there are two middle terms, $T_5$ and $T_6$.\n$T_5 = {}^9C_4 (2x)^5\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^4 = 126 \\cdot 2x = 252x$.\n$T_6 = -{}^9C_5 (2x)^4\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)^5 = -126 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x} = -\\frac{63}{x}$.\nProduct $= 252 \\times (-63) = -15876$, which is free of $x$. Correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The triangular arrangement of binomial coefficients called Meru Prastara was given by Pingla in which work?","image":"","options":["Aryabhatiya","Chhanda shastra","Lilavati","Trate du triange arithmetic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT's historical note says Pingla gave the Meru-Prastara in his book Chhanda shastra (200 B.C.).\nIt covered the coefficients of $(x+y)^n$ for $0 \\le n \\le 7$.\nOption (4) is tempting but wrong: Trate du triange arithmetic is Pascal's work, published in 1665.\nAryabhatiya and Lilavati are other Indian mathematical texts and are not linked to this diagram.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-BINOMIAL-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Binomial Theorem","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Binomial Theorem","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $(x+2)^6$ is","image":"","options":["160","120","240","20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"General term: $T_{r+1} = {}^6C_r\\, x^{6-r}\\, 2^r$.\nFor $x^3$ we need $6-r=3$, so $r=3$.\nCoefficient $= {}^6C_3 \\cdot 2^3 = 20 \\times 8 = 160$.\nThe tempting value 20 is only ${}^6C_3$; it misses the factor $2^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 7 · Binomial Theorem","difficulty":"easy"}]});
